## Current status

Keep a running log here of what's built, what's in progress, and non-obvious decisions and
fixes as you go -- this is what lets a new session pick up context instead of re-deriving it.
A good entry names the date, what changed, why, and any gotcha a future session would
otherwise rediscover the hard way. Delete this paragraph once you've written your first
real entries.

---

## What to do first in a new session

1. Read this file (done if you're here).
2. `careeros status` to see current DB state.
3. If bank_records is 0, run `careeros sync`. If jobs is 0 or stale, run `careeros scan`.
4. Ask what you want to work on. Don't assume.

---

## Commands cheatsheet

```
# Setup (one time; assumes .venv is already created)
source .venv/bin/activate

# Every session
careeros status                    # DB row counts
careeros sync                      # pull Bank from Notion
careeros scan                      # fetch open jobs across supported companies
careeros jobs list                 # new-status jobs
careeros jobs list --status saved  # shortlist
careeros jobs view <id>            # full details
careeros jobs save <id>            # mark saved
careeros jobs reject <id>          # mark rejected
careeros jobs mark-applied <id>    # mark applied
careeros jobs stage <id> <stage>   # set post-application stage -- define your own
                                    # stage vocabulary and keep jobs.VALID_STAGES in sync
careeros jobs set-remote-type <id> <type>  # correct remote/hybrid/onsite/unknown
careeros jobs clear                # delete all new-status jobs (preserves triaged)
careeros coach analyze <id>        # match Bank tech/tools against a job's description
careeros coach compare             # rank saved jobs by Bank match strength

# Data / config
careeros facts show
careeros facts reload              # after editing data/facts.yaml
careeros companies show
careeros companies reload          # after editing data/companies.yaml (upserts by slug,
                                    # safe to run anytime)

# Vetting (usually done in Notion directly; this is the local fallback)
careeros vet [--all]

# Application engine (Phase 4)
careeros tailor <job_id>                              # build a tailoring package
careeros tailor-budget <bullets_file>                  # character-count check
careeros resume-build <job_id> --content <json>        # write drafted content into a
                                                        # copy of your resume template
careeros cover-letter-build <job_id> --content <json>  # same, for the cover letter
```

